# SQL testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to testing raw
`.sql` — migrations, schema, and hand-written query files — across
Postgres, MySQL, and generic SQL engines. Applies only to `*.sql` files;
testing a query or migration issued from application code (a language's
ORM test helpers) is covered by that language's own stack pack, and
should still follow the transactional-wrapping and fixture discipline
below where that language's test suite talks to a real database.

## Test isolation

- Wrap each test in a transaction that is rolled back at teardown
  (`BEGIN` before the test, `ROLLBACK` after) rather than truncating and
  re-seeding tables between tests — rollback is faster and guarantees no
  leftover state regardless of what the test wrote.
- When a test must exercise something a transaction can't roll back
  cleanly (`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`, an advisory-lock scenario, a
  cross-transaction concurrency test), isolate it in its own
  database/schema that is torn down and recreated instead of relying on
  transactional rollback for that one case.
- Never point a test suite at a shared development or staging database —
  a fixed, disposable test database (a local container, an ephemeral
  schema per CI run) so tests don't observe or clobber each other's data.

## Fixtures and seed data

- Seed only the rows a given test actually needs, built through the same
  helper/factory the rest of the suite uses — do not duplicate a large
  shared seed script per test file, and do not depend on a global fixture
  a different test also mutates.
- Name fixture data so a failure is legible (`user_with_expired_token`,
  not `test_user_3`) — a table-driven or parameterized test case should
  carry a descriptive case name for the same reason.
- A fixture representing a timestamp or "now"-relative value is
  constructed relative to a fixed, injected clock/reference time in the
  test, not `NOW()`/`CURRENT_TIMESTAMP` evaluated live — a live clock
  value makes boundary cases (rows that just expired, rows created "just
  now") flaky depending on when the test happens to run.

## Testing migrations

- Test a migration's `up` path against a database seeded to look like
  production before the change: representative row counts for anything
  the migration scans or backfills, not an empty table where every
  approach looks instant.
- Test the reverse/`down` path when the migration states one — running
  `up` then `down` should leave the schema equivalent to before, and
  running `up` twice (or `down` twice) should not error when the
  migration was written to be idempotent.
- For a batched backfill migration, test with a row count large enough to
  exercise at least two batches, and assert every row was updated exactly
  once (no row skipped at a batch boundary, none processed twice in a way
  that would double-apply a non-idempotent update).

## Testing queries

- Assert on the query's actual result set (rows, values, ordering when
  the query specifies `ORDER BY`), not merely that it executed without
  error — a query testing only "did not throw" would pass even after a
  regression silently returns zero or the wrong rows.
- For a query added to fix an N+1, assert the query count too (most
  ORMs/test harnesses expose a query counter or log) — a regression test
  for an N+1 fix should fail again if the fix regresses back to per-row
  queries, not just check the final result shape.
- Test the empty-result case (a `WHERE` that matches nothing) and the
  boundary case for any range condition (`created_at >= X`) explicitly —
  these are exactly where an off-by-one in a range filter or a `JOIN`
  that should have been a `LEFT JOIN` shows up.

## Verification

- Run the full suite against the transactional-rollback isolation
  described above before considering a change to `.sql` test fixtures or
  migration tests done — a test that passes in isolation but leaves state
  for the next test to trip over is not actually isolated.
